  "597056": [
   {
    "old": "<img class=\"internal\" alt=\"Four Lewis structures that students drew, each in its own box. (a) Methanol, CH3OH, as drawn: carbon bonded to three H and to O, and the O bonded to one H with three lone pairs. Corrected: the O has two lone pairs. Error: too many electrons; the drawing uses 16 electrons but methanol has 14. (b) Formaldehyde, H2C=O: carbon bonded to two H and double-bonded to O, which has two lone pairs. This one is correct: 12 electrons, carbon has four bonds, oxygen has two bonds and two lone pairs. (c) Ethanethiol, CH3CH2SH, as drawn: the sulfur has two bonds and only one lone pair. Corrected: the sulfur has two lone pairs. Error: octet not satisfied; S has only 6 electrons. (d) Methylamine, as drawn: the nitrogen is bonded to the carbon and to three H, with no lone pair. Corrected: CH3NH2, nitrogen bonded to carbon and two H with one lone pair. Error: four bonds and no lone pair is the pattern of a positive nitrogen, as in CH3NH3+.\" width=\"760\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto;\" src=\"/@api/deki/files/512789/fig-p597056-lewis-errors.svg\" fileid=\"512789\" />",
