    "old": "<img class=\"internal\" alt=\"Two side-by-side comparisons of hydrogen bonding. Left: two acetic acid molecules, CH3COOH, face each other head to head. The O-H hydrogen of each acid is joined by a dotted hydrogen bond to the C=O oxygen of the other acid, closing a ring, so the pair is held by two hydrogen bonds. Right: two ethanol molecules, CH3CH2OH; the O-H hydrogen of one is joined by a single dotted hydrogen bond to the oxygen of the other, so the pair is held by one hydrogen bond. Lone pairs are not shown.\" width=\"760\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto;\" src=\"/@api/deki/files/512783/fig-p597111-acid-dimer.svg\" fileid=\"512783\" />",
