    "old": "<img class=\"internal\" alt=\"Three red blood cells, each in a different salt solution. Left, hypotonic, 0.45 percent NaCl: blue arrows point from the solution into the cell; water moves in, and the cell is drawn swollen and round because it swells and may burst. Middle, isotonic, 0.9 percent NaCl (normal saline): pairs of arrows point both in and out, showing no net movement of water; the cell is a normal biconcave disc with a pale center. Right, hypertonic, 3 percent NaCl: arrows point out of the cell; water moves out, and the cell is drawn small with a spiky, crenated edge because it shrinks.\" width=\"760\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto;\" src=\"/@api/deki/files/512784/fig-p597148-rbc-tonicity.svg\" fileid=\"512784\" />",
